 de No. 2 in F Major\, Op. 38 (1839)<br /></em>Mussorgsky: <em>Pictures at
  an Exhibition (1874)<br /></em><br /><strong>About the Artist</strong><b
 r /><br />  American pianist <strong>Gregory Sioles</strong> is praised f
 or passionate\, stylish\, and insightful performances. Sioles has been th
 e recipient of numerous honors and awards including a Fulbright Scholarsh
 ip and an Atwater Kent Grand Prize. He has performed on three continents 
 at such venues as the Purcell Room (London)\, Amerika Haus (Berlin)\, and
  the Shanghai Conservatory. Following his London debut\, he made solo app
 earances in Spain\, Germany\, Switzerland\, and Scotland. In Spain and in
  London\, he served as the teaching assistant for the legendary pedagogue
  Maria Curcio. In addition to Curcio\, he studied with György Sebők and
  Aube Tzerko. Sioles has performed throughout the United States as a reci
 talist and soloist with orchestra\, and been a featured artist on Nationa
 l Public Radio. An avid chamber player\, he has collaborated with numerou
 s artists\, including Edgar Meyer\, Barry Tuckwell\, the Guarneri String 
 Quartet\, and members of the Baltimore and National symphony orchestras. 
 As part of the Washington D.C. music scene for over a decade (1991-2003)\
 , he performed solo and chamber recitals in many of the city's important 
 halls\, including the Kennedy Center\, the National Gallery\, the Phillip
 s Collection\, the Cosmos Club\, Strathmore Hall\, and the French Embassy
 . Recent international engagements have taken him to Taiwan\, where he pe
 rformed in five cities including Kaohsiung and Taipei\, and to Panama and
  Costa Rica\, where he gave chamber and solo performances and taught mast
 er classes. From 2019 to 2024\, Sioles taught at the Indiana University J
 acobs School of Music. Previously\, he taught at Louisiana State Universi
 ty\, the University of Maryland\, and Levine Music in Washington\, D.C. H
 e records for Centaur Records.</p>
